# STIME(2)

## NAME

stime - set time

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <sys/types.h>

int stime(time_t t);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Stime* sets the system's idea of the time and date. *T* is measured in seconds from 0000 GMT
Jan 1, 1970. Only the super-user may use this call.

**Note:** v7's page took a POINTER here, and so did this one and the prototype in
lib/libc/sys/syscalls.tbl until date(1) became the first caller there had ever been. It is a
VALUE on this machine, for the reason the notes below give: the gate reads one word out of the
argument list and the generated stub dereferences nothing.

## SEE ALSO

date(1), time(2), ctime(3)

## DIAGNOSTICS

Zero is returned if the time was set; -1 if user is not the super-user.

## BESM-6 NOTES

**time_t is one word - 41 bits of seconds, so there is no 2038 problem - and the gate shed an
argument because of it.** v7's **time_t** was two PDP-11 words and its gate took both. See
lseek(2), which collapsed the same way, and intro(2) for why the argument count is a contract
between three places and not one.

## ASSEMBLER

**Note:** v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here - a decimal call number and a `sys name; arg ...`
line. Neither is how a call is made on this machine. The gate is one extracode - **$77
SYS_stime** - and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the error number
in r14.

**The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.** The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is
the only place a syscall number appears on the user side. See intro(2) for the whole contract.
